<div class="blurb">
<div class="header"><h2>in plain english</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>Someone reading the previous section might say, "explain that to me in plain english." So here it is, with no chemical formulas.</p>

<p><span class="highlight">The FDA's one sentence is sloppy.</span> The FDA writes: <em>"MSG occurs naturally in many foods, such as tomatoes and cheeses."</em> But MSG &mdash; <span class="highlight">monosodium glutamate</span> &mdash; is a <em>factory-made seasoning</em>. The FDA says so on the very same page: it is "produced by the fermentation of starch, sugar beets, sugar cane or molasses."</p>

<p>A thing cannot be both a factory product <em>and</em> something that occurs naturally in a tomato. The FDA is using one word, MSG, to mean two different things.</p>

<h3>the two things the fda is mixing up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>MSG</strong> &mdash; a specific, factory-made white powder. It is glutamate paired with a sodium partner, crystallized and sold as a flavor enhancer. It has a food additive number: E621.</li>
<li><strong>Glutamate</strong> &mdash; an amino acid that is in lots of foods, including tomatoes and cheeses. In a tomato, this glutamate is paired with potassium, calcium, or just floating around as the free acid. It is not MSG.</li>
</ul>
<p>The accurate sentence would be: <em>"Glutamate occurs naturally in tomatoes and cheeses. MSG is the factory-made sodium salt of that amino acid."</em></p>

<h3>a useful analogy</h3>
<p>Sodium and chlorine both occur naturally in seawater. Table salt &mdash; sodium chloride &mdash; is what you get when you refine seawater into a specific crystalline product. Saying "salt occurs naturally in seawater" is loose &mdash; the sea does not contain the salt you sprinkle on fries, only the raw ingredients.</p>
<p>Tomatoes contain the raw ingredient (glutamate). MSG is the refined, factory-made form. <span class="highlight">Tomatoes do not contain MSG.</span></p>

<h3>the fda contradicts itself</h3>
<p>On the very same page where it says MSG "occurs naturally" in tomatoes, the FDA answers a different question, <em>"What's the difference between MSG and glutamate in food?"</em>, and says this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Proteins are made up of amino acids, including glutamic acid. When proteins are digested, they release free glutamate, which is chemically the same as the glutamate in MSG." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>That sentence is correct &mdash; and it is also the FDA's own way of saying glutamate and MSG are different things. The first sentence is sloppy; this one is careful. They cannot both be right.</p>

<p>The FDA's labeling rule settles it too: if MSG has been <em>added</em> to a food, it has to be listed on the ingredient panel as "monosodium glutamate." Whole tomatoes and whole cheeses never list it &mdash; because none is added <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup>. The FDA treats MSG as something added, not as something that occurs.</p>

<h3>the bottom line</h3>
<p>Tomatoes and cheeses contain glutamate &mdash; the same amino acid that gives MSG its flavor. They do not contain MSG &mdash; the factory-made sodium salt. The FDA's "occurs naturally" sentence is wrong, and the FDA's own other sentences prove it.</p>

<p><em>One honest footnote:</em> once you swallow either one, your body sees the same glutamate molecule &mdash; the FDA's page says so. So this section is about chemistry, terminology, and labeling &mdash; not a claim that the glutamate in a tomato is a different chemical from the glutamate in MSG. What <em>is</em> different is the dose, the concentration, and the fact that added MSG never has to be labeled.</p>

